WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.533
Kafka.

2
00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:04.833
Kafka is the backbone of real-time pipelines.

3
00:00:04.833 --> 00:00:09.300
The idea is simple: an append-only log that many readers can share.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.167
Kafka is a distributed append-only log.

5
00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:16.308
Producers append messages to named topics.

6
00:00:16.308 --> 00:00:19.554
Messages are never changed or deleted when read.

7
00:00:19.554 --> 00:00:22.733
They are only appended, and much later expired.

8
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:25.967
Each topic splits into partitions.

9
00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:31.033
Partitions are independent ordered logs, and that is where parallelism comes from.

10
00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:37.267
Messages with the same key always land in the same partition, so order is preserved per key.

11
00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:40.333
Different partitions can be consumed in parallel.

12
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:44.467
Here is the crucial inversion versus a classic queue.

13
00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:48.681
Consumers track their own position in the log, called the offset.

14
00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:51.933
One group, the anomaly detector, has read up to three.

15
00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:55.477
Another group, the warehouse loader, is already at six.

16
00:00:55.477 --> 00:00:57.600
The broker does not push and forget.

17
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.633
Here it is in code, trimmed down.

18
00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:06.267
The producer sends a message with a key, so every NOAA message lands in the same partition.

19
00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:10.833
The consumer joins a group by name, and Kafka remembers that group's place.

20
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.100
As we loop, the offset advances.

21
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.200
Here is why this design matters.

22
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.967
A telemetry stream feeds two consumer groups: an anomaly detector and a warehouse loader.

23
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.451
The detector crashes on Friday night.

24
00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:26.400
The loader carries on, untouched.

25
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:33.154
On Monday, the detector restarts at its committed offset and works through the weekend backlog in order.

26
00:01:33.154 --> 00:01:34.333
Zero messages lost.

27
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:36.380
Let's recap.

28
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.133
Kafka is a distributed append-only log.

29
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:42.367
Partitions give parallelism, with per-key order.

30
00:01:42.667 --> 00:01:44.333
Consumers track their own offset.

31
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:47.101
Restart to resume, rewind to replay.

32
00:01:47.101 --> 00:01:48.267
Next up: Airflow.
